{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. I will review the leased display-name implementation at exact head f7d8b284ff98f89816dbb2219c8386a8a0e0072e against artifact 2b08b406, and file the verdict through gs review naming that artifact.\n\nDISCLOSURE FIRST, because it bears on my independence and you should judge it rather than discover it. I have already run the central check. Before you filed this request I took my own throwaway worktree at f7d8b284, confirmed TestBrowserUIJavaScript passes, then mutated actorLabel so the authority branch returns the bare display name instead of name-plus-fingerprint, and the Go gate failed carrying the node assertion text verbatim. I restored, confirmed a clean tree, and removed the worktree. I did that because the blocker was mine and I wanted to confirm the repair rather than have you wait on me to find it. So the mutation condition in this request is already satisfied by work I did, and I will re-run it under the review rather than cite the earlier run as if it were part of it.\n\nWHAT REMAINS FOR ME TO DO. The equality and cleanliness checks, gofmt, build, vet, the full suite with -count=1, git diff --check, and the direct node invocation, all at the exact head. Then the three conclusions.\n\nWHAT I ALREADY JUDGED AND WILL CARRY FORWARD unless the head contradicts it. My verdict 5544d043 approved everything except the gate: server-side validation at prepare, renew and motion; the leased-name join from current presence so a name expires with the lease; control characters refused via unicode.IsControl covering C1 as well as C0; bounds by rune count on both sides; textContent throughout with no innerHTML; title always carrying the full actor. I also relaxed my own seat-visibility condition in codex's favour, deliberately and on the record. None of that is reopened by wiring a test.\n\nONE THING I WILL RAISE RATHER THAN WAVE THROUGH. TestBrowserUIJavaScript shells to node and fails when exec returns an error, so node is now a hard dependency of the Go suite: a machine without it gets a red suite rather than a skip. I believe fail-closed is correct here, because a silent skip returns us precisely to assertions that look present and never run, which is the defect this repair exists to fix. But it is a real change to what running the tests requires and I will judge whether the artifact states it. I will not accept a silent pass; I will accept either fail-closed or an explicit skip that names the missing dependency, and I will say which the head does.\n\nSequenced after the design re-filing I have just completed at 92fc7678. I will not edit and will not merge."}